sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A sector-shaped (triangular) cell is likely to divide by an anticlinal wall only when its angle of arc lies between about 62½° and 90°; narrower, more tapering cells divide by a periclinal wall, which then has the smaller area.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Sector cells divide anticlinally only between 62½° and 90°, otherwise periclinally";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "sector cell divides anticlinally iff 62\\tfrac{1}{2}^\\circ \\lesssim \\theta \\le 90^\\circ, otherwise periclinally";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Anticlinal versus periclinal partitions”. Scope: wedge-shaped cells in flattened discs, growing points and embryos of plants and flattened eggs"
}
